/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-4","number":4,"title":"Instrument Pads","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"4.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-4-1-101","code":"4.1","title":"4.1","blurb":"4.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l4-1-1","title":"4.1.1 — Three entry paths: empty Add Clip, track header, double-tap clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.1 — Three entry paths: empty Add Clip, track header, double-tap clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Instrument View from Session three different ways and know which path also creates a clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Instrument View is where you play pads, capture and edit notes, and shape the Track Preset. You do not stay in Session to do that work. From Session View you have three documented entries."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap the <strong>Add Clip</strong> icon in an empty clip slot. That path both creates a new clip and takes you into Instrument View. Tap the <strong>Track</strong> header when you want the same view without committing a new empty clip first. Double-tap an existing clip when you want to land on that clip’s notes and parameters."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use the header or a double-tap when the clip already exists and you only need to play or edit. Use <strong>Add Clip</strong> when the slot is empty and you are ready to start a loop. All three land you in the same view: Track Preset at the top, Clip Preview, pads, and the bottom actions row."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set so you are looking at Session View.","Tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> in an empty slot on the drum track. Confirm Instrument View opens and a clip exists in that slot when you later return.","Tap the back arrow to the left of the Set name to return to Session.","Tap the same track’s <strong>Track</strong> header. Confirm Instrument View opens again.","Return to Session. Double-tap the clip you just created. Confirm Instrument View opens on that clip.","Close this page and name the three paths from memory, then do each once more."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which entry also creates a clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tapping <strong>Add Clip</strong> in an empty slot.","How do you open Instrument View on a clip that already exists without adding another clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Double-tap the clip, or tap the <strong>Track</strong> header.","What three regions does Instrument View always show? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Track Preset section, the Clip Preview window, and the pads."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 Instrument View (three entries; view contents)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 Clips (<strong>Add Clip</strong> opens Instrument View)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On three different tracks, enter Instrument View once via <strong>Add Clip</strong>, once via the <strong>Track</strong> header, and once via double-tap. Speak the path you used before you tap."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-1-2","title":"4.1.2 — Track Preset name and tabs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.2 — Track Preset name and tabs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Read the Track Preset name and tabs so you know which instrument and which two audio effects sit on the track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Once Instrument View is open, the name of the currently loaded <strong>Track Preset</strong> sits at the top. Immediately below that name is the Track Preset section: tabs that hold the instrument’s device parameters and the two audio effects that ship with every preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"A Track Preset is one instrument plus two audio effects. The first tab shows the instrument, marked with a distinct icon: <strong>Drift</strong> (subtractive), <strong>Drum Rack</strong> (16 pads, each a Drum Sampler plus pad FX send and kit FX), <strong>Melodic Sampler</strong> (one sample repitched across the pads), or <strong>Wavetable</strong>. On Drum Rack the effect tabs are <strong>Pad FX</strong> then <strong>Kit FX</strong>. On Drift, Melodic Sampler, and Wavetable they are <strong>FX1</strong> then <strong>FX2</strong>, in series."},{"type":"prose","html":"Each new track loads a random Track Preset. The name is the handle you tap later to open the browser. Do not treat the tabs as extra tracks; they are the sound of this one track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on the first default track (tap the <strong>Track</strong> header).","Read the Track Preset name at the top and the icon in the first tab. Name the instrument family out loud: Drift, Drum Rack, Melodic Sampler, or Wavetable.","Tap through the remaining tabs. On a Drum Rack, find <strong>Pad FX</strong> and <strong>Kit FX</strong>. On a melodic or synth track, find <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong>.","Open the second default track the same way and compare the tab labels.","Return to the first track without reading this page and point to: name, instrument tab, two effect tabs."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a Track Preset contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> One instrument with adjustable parameters and two audio effects.","What are the two effect-tab names on a Drum Rack versus a Drift track? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drum Rack: <strong>Pad FX</strong> and <strong>Kit FX</strong>. Drift: <strong>FX1</strong> and <strong>FX2</strong>.","Where is the loaded preset’s name shown? <strong>Answer:</strong> At the top of Instrument View, above the tabs."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 (name at top; Track Preset section)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 Track Presets (random preset; four instruments; two effects)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (Pad FX / Kit FX vs FX1 / FX2)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a three-line card for the open Set: track name, instrument family, and the two effect-tab labels. Do this for every track you currently have."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-1-3","title":"4.1.3 — Clip Preview window","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.3 — Clip Preview window","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the Clip Preview window to see captured notes and to open the MIDI Editor."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Instrument View puts device parameters front and center. Directly below that area sits the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> window. It displays the notes in the current clip as a compact overview — the same notes you later edit in detail."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> window to open the MIDI Editor. That editor is still part of Instrument View: time ruler, loop brace, and a grid of drum hits or pitches. You do not leave the track to get there. Long-press on the preview is a different gesture and is the next lesson; a single tap is the editor."},{"type":"prose","html":"Until notes exist, the preview is mostly empty. That is expected. After you play and capture (Level 5), the preview fills so you can confirm the take without opening the full editor every time. Automation you capture later also draws here: a dotted line while pending, a solid line once stored."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on a track that already has a clip (create one with <strong>Add Clip</strong> if the slot is empty).","Find the <strong>Clip Preview</strong> window between the Track Preset tabs and the pads.","Tap the preview once. Confirm the MIDI Editor opens.","Tap back out of the editor so you see the compact preview again.","Play a few pads and watch whether anything appears in the preview (notes you have not yet committed may show as you play; committing them is Level 5).","Close this page, reopen Instrument View, and point to the preview before you tap it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does a single tap on Clip Preview do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It opens the MIDI Editor.","Where does Clip Preview sit in Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> Below the Track Preset section and above the pads.","Is the MIDI Editor a separate app screen outside Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The manual treats it as part of Instrument View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 (Clip Preview displays notes and opens the MIDI Editor)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2 (preview below parameters; tap to open the editor)","Ableton Note Manual §7.5 (automation appears in Clip Preview)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap into the MIDI Editor and back to the compact preview five times on the same clip. Stop when you can find the preview without hunting."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-1-4","title":"4.1.4 — Long-press preview: duplicate/crop/delete/create clip","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.4 — Long-press preview: duplicate/crop/delete/create clip","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Long-press Clip Preview to duplicate, crop, or delete the loop, or to create a clip when none exists yet."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A tap on <strong>Clip Preview</strong> opens the MIDI Editor. A long-press on the same window opens a different menu. The manual lists these commands: duplicate the loop, crop the loop, delete the loop, and create a clip if one has not been added on the track yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this menu when the loop itself is the object — double the material, trim to the loop, throw the loop away — without first diving into individual notes. <strong>Create a clip</strong> is the escape hatch if you arrived via the <strong>Track</strong> header and still have no clip on the track."},{"type":"prose","html":"The same family of loop commands also appears later on the MIDI Editor loop brace (duplicate, crop, zoom). This lesson stays on the preview long-press so you have a Session-adjacent way to act on the loop. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not list extra preview-long-press labels beyond duplicate, crop, delete, and create clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View from a <strong>Track</strong> header on a track with no clip in the current slot.","Long-press <strong>Clip Preview</strong>. If <strong>create a clip</strong> is offered, use it, then confirm a clip now exists.","Play and commit a short idea later in Level 5, or add a Fixed Length clip with the plus icon so the preview has a loop to act on.","Long-press the preview again. Identify <strong>duplicate</strong>, <strong>crop</strong>, and <strong>delete</strong> without firing delete until you have a spare take.","Duplicate once. Confirm you still have a loop to play.","Recite the four long-press commands from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the difference between tap and long-press on Clip Preview? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap opens the MIDI Editor. Long-press opens loop commands (duplicate / crop / delete / create clip).","When does the create-clip command appear? <strong>Answer:</strong> When a clip has not been added on the track yet.","Does this long-press replace the MIDI Editor loop brace? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The loop brace has its own duplicate / crop / zoom commands later."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2 (long-press preview: duplicate, crop, delete, create clip)","Ableton Note Manual §6 (tap preview opens the MIDI Editor)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a throwaway clip, long-press the preview and duplicate the loop once. Do not delete your only take. Write the four command names on paper."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-1-5","title":"4.1.5 — Pads region","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.5 — Pads region","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the pads region, play it, and know the default grid changes with the instrument type."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The lower part of Instrument View is the pads — the playable surface you use to perform. Performances you play here are what Capture later stores in clips. You do not need a clip already playing in order to hear a pad; the instrument sounds when you tap."},{"type":"prose","html":"Default layout depends on the Track Preset. A <strong>Drum Rack</strong> uses a <strong>4×4</strong> grid (16 pads). A melodic instrument — Drift, Melodic Sampler, Wavetable — uses a <strong>5×5</strong> grid by default. You can later switch a drum track to <strong>Single</strong> or a melodic track to <strong>Keys</strong>, but those are Pad Performance Options, not the factory default."},{"type":"prose","html":"On larger screens, Note 1.4+ shows more pads. That is a display change, not a new instrument. Play first, change layout second. The pads sit below Clip Preview; the Pad Performance Options control sits below the pads."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on the Drum Rack track from the <strong>Track</strong> header.","Play several pads. Count the grid: four across, four down.","Return to Session and open a melodic track (bass or lead).","Play that grid. Confirm it is the 5×5 default, not 4×4.","Point to three stacked regions without looking at this page: Track Preset tabs, Clip Preview, pads."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the default drum pad grid? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>4×4</strong> (16 pads).","What is the default melodic pad grid? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>5×5</strong>.","Where do the pads sit relative to Clip Preview? <strong>Answer:</strong> Below the Clip Preview window."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 (pads as part of Instrument View; play and capture)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Drum Rack 4×4 default; melodic 5×5 default)","Note Release Notes 1.4 (more pads on larger screens)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play a one-hand groove on the drum 4×4, then a short lick on a melodic 5×5. Do not change Pad Performance Options yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-1-6","title":"4.1.6 — Bottom actions row map","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.6 — Bottom actions row map","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Name every control on the Instrument View bottom actions row before you rely on any one of them."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The bottom of Instrument View holds a dedicated actions row. The manual lists five items, in this teaching order: <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong> arrows, the <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> button, <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong>, the <strong>Note Quantize</strong> button, and <strong>Play/Stop</strong> for global transport."},{"type":"prose","html":"These are not mixer faders and not the Session scene numbers. They are the actions you use while playing and capturing on this track. <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong> apply to changes in the Set. <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> opens a sidebar whose contents change with Drum Rack versus melodic presets. <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> copies the open clip into the next available Session slot and switches playback to that copy. <strong>Note Quantize</strong> snaps notes to a musical grid. <strong>Play/Stop</strong> starts or stops the Set’s global transport."},{"type":"prose","html":"Learn the map now so later lessons can say “tap <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong>” without you hunting. Capture’s <strong>Add</strong> / <strong>Capture</strong> / <strong>Create Clip</strong> and the <strong>X</strong> clear control appear in the clip/pad area when there is something to commit or discard; they are not the same five actions."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on any track.","Point to each bottom control and name it: <strong>Undo</strong>, <strong>Redo</strong>, <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>, <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong>, <strong>Note Quantize</strong>, <strong>Play/Stop</strong>.","Tap <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> once so you see the sidebar, then close it.","Tap <strong>Play/Stop</strong> once to start transport, then again to stop. Confirm clips or the metronome (if enabled) follow the global transport.","Cover the screen with your hand, uncover it, and name the row left to right."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which bottom button opens layout, Repeat, and related performance controls? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>.","What does <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> do besides copying? <strong>Answer:</strong> It places the copy in the next available Session slot and switches playback to that clip.","Does <strong>Play/Stop</strong> in Instrument View control only this track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It starts or stops global transport."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 Instrument View Actions (all five controls)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"From Session, enter Instrument View and tap each bottom control once (skip Quantize if the clip has no notes you care about). Write the five names from memory."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-1-7","title":"4.1.7 — Browser open by tapping instrument/sample/effect names","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.7 — Browser open by tapping instrument/sample/effect names","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Note’s browser from Instrument View by tapping a preset, sample, or effect name."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The browser lives inside Instrument View. You open it by tapping any <strong>instrument</strong>, <strong>sample</strong>, or <strong>effect</strong> name on the track — including the Track Preset name at the top. From there you swap instruments, samples, effects, or entire Track Presets, mark favorites, and import your own recordings."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap a preset name in the list to hear an audio preview. You can also play the pads in the Pad View below the list to audition the sound on the grid. Tap <strong>Done</strong> to close the browser and load the selected preset. Search and the <strong>Filters</strong> icon (including <strong>Favorite</strong>) sit at the top of the browser. Saving a tweaked sound as a user preset is a later instrument-level skill; this lesson is only the open-and-close path."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not look for a separate “Browser” app icon on the Session grid. The names on the track are the doors."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on a melodic or drum track.","Tap the Track Preset name at the top. Confirm the browser opens.","Tap one other preset in the same category so you hear the preview. Play a pad underneath the list.","Tap <strong>Done</strong> to load that preset, or tap back if you only wanted to look.","Tap an effect name in <strong>FX1</strong> / <strong>FX2</strong> or <strong>Pad FX</strong> / <strong>Kit FX</strong> and confirm the effect browser opens. Close it with <strong>Done</strong> or by leaving without changing.","Reopen the browser from the preset name without reading this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How do you open the browser from Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> Tap any instrument, sample, or effect name (including the Track Preset name).","How do you audition a listed preset besides the automatic preview? <strong>Answer:</strong> Play the pads in the Pad View below the list.","What button closes the browser and loads the selection? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Done</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6 (open browser from instrument, sample, or effect name)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1.1 Browsing Presets (preview, pads under the list, <strong>Done</strong>, search, Filters, favorites)","Ableton Note Manual §7.4 (tap an effect name to swap it)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Swap one effect on a throwaway track, then swap it back. Use only name-taps to enter the browser."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-1-8","title":"4.1.8 — Return to Session without losing place","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.1.8 — Return to Session without losing place","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.1 — Entering and reading Instrument View</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Instrument View with the back arrow and land in Session on the same open Set, not in the Sets Library."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The back arrow to the left of the Set name is context-sensitive. In Session View it returns you to the <strong>Sets Library</strong>. In Instrument View the same visual control returns you to <strong>Session View</strong>. That is how you leave the pads without closing the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Set stays open. Clips you already created remain in their slots. Pad Performance Options are stored per track with the Set, so layout and Repeat choices survive a trip back to Session. One thing does not survive: the MIDI Editor clears its note selection when you close the editor or exit Instrument View."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not tap the arrow twice in a row unless you intend to leave the Set for the library. One tap from Instrument View is enough."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["From Session, tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> on any track so you are in Instrument View.","Note the Set name in the top bar.","Tap the back arrow once. Confirm you see the Session grid of this Set, not the Sets Library.","Re-enter via the <strong>Track</strong> header, then leave again with one tap.","From Session, tap the back arrow once more and notice that this second tap does go to the library. Reopen the Set.","Repeat the Instrument View → one-tap-back path from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where does one back-arrow tap from Instrument View take you? <strong>Answer:</strong> Session View of the same Set.","Where does one back-arrow tap from Session View take you? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Sets Library.","What editor state is explicitly cleared when you exit Instrument View? <strong>Answer:</strong> MIDI Editor note selection."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §4 (Session back arrow → Sets Library)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (Instrument View back arrow → Session View)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Pad Performance state saved with the Set)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3 (note selection cleared when you exit Instrument View)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enter and leave Instrument View ten times, using a different track every other time. Stop if you ever land in the library by accident; reset and continue."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-1-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-4-2-102","code":"4.2","title":"4.2","blurb":"4.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l4-2-1","title":"4.2.1 — Undo / Redo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.1 — Undo / Redo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.2 — Bottom actions fluency</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Undo a change and redo it from the Instrument View arrows so a bad tap is not a lost take."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The leftmost Instrument View actions are the <strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong> arrows. They undo or redo any changes you make in your Set — not only notes. A preset swap, a deleted clip, or a parameter nudge is in the same history."},{"type":"prose","html":"These arrows also exist on the Session View actions row. You do not have to stay in Instrument View to step backward. If you map a MIDI controller later, <strong>Undo</strong> and <strong>Redo</strong> are among the dedicated Map targets, together with Capture, Clear Capture, Start/Stop, and Quantize."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not state how many undo steps Note keeps. Do not assume a desktop-DAW history depth."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use <strong>Undo</strong> immediately when a capture or a pad hit is wrong. Use <strong>Redo</strong> if the undo itself was the mistake. Do not delete a clip to “undo” a take when the arrow will do."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on any track.","Change something small: drag one device knob, or tap a different pad so you can hear a new hit.","Tap <strong>Undo</strong>. Confirm the change reverses.","Tap <strong>Redo</strong>. Confirm the change returns.","Return to Session, change a mixer slider or mute, then use the Session <strong>Undo</strong> arrow.","Repeat the Instrument View undo/redo pair from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What do the Instrument View arrows undo? <strong>Answer:</strong> Any changes you make in the Set, not only notes.","Do you have to stay in Instrument View to undo? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Session View also has <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong>.","How many undo steps does the manual promise? <strong>Answer:</strong> It does not say. Treat depth as unknown."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (Undo/Redo arrows)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 (Session View Undo/Redo)","Ableton Note Manual §2.3.5 (Undo and Redo are MIDI-mappable)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make three deliberate changes (knob, mute, preset preview you then <strong>Done</strong>). Undo all three, then Redo one. Listen after each tap."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-2-2","title":"4.2.2 — Pad Performance Options button","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.2 — Pad Performance Options button","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.2 — Bottom actions fluency</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the Pad Performance Options sidebar and see that its contents change with Drum Rack versus melodic tracks."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> button sits on the Instrument View bottom row. Tap it and a sidebar opens with the options available for the current Track Preset."},{"type":"prose","html":"On a <strong>Drum Rack</strong> you get <strong>Pads</strong> (the default 4×4), <strong>Single</strong> (one large velocity surface), and <strong>Repeat</strong> (beat-division submenu, including <strong>OFF</strong>). On a melodic instrument you get <strong>Pads</strong> (the default 5×5), <strong>Keys</strong> (two-octave keyboard), <strong>Octave</strong>, and <strong>Repeat</strong>. Note 1.2+ also places <strong>Arpeggiator</strong> options in the sidebar next to the pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"The sidebar is how you change the playable surface without leaving Instrument View. The state of these options is per track and saved with the Set. Open the button to look in this lesson; later modules drill each option. Do not hunt for Repeat or Keys in Set Settings — they are not there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on the Drum Rack track.","Tap <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>. Read the drum list: <strong>Pads</strong>, <strong>Single</strong>, <strong>Repeat</strong>.","Close the sidebar. Return to Session and open a melodic track.","Tap <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> again. Read the melodic list: <strong>Pads</strong>, <strong>Keys</strong>, <strong>Octave</strong>, <strong>Repeat</strong>. If your version is 1.2 or later, also look for <strong>Arpeggiator</strong>.","Leave both tracks on their default <strong>Pads</strong> layout.","From memory, say which three drum options and which four (or five) melodic options the sidebar holds."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you open Pad Performance Options? <strong>Answer:</strong> The button on the Instrument View bottom actions row (the icon below the pads).","Which option appears only on Drum Racks? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Single</strong>.","Which option appears on melodic tracks but not as a drum layout? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Keys</strong> (and <strong>Octave</strong> as a pitch-range control)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (button opens a sidebar that depends on the Track Preset)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (drum vs melodic option lists; state saved with the Set)","Note Release Notes 1.2 (Arpeggiator options in the sidebar)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open the sidebar on every track in the Set. Write “drum” or “melodic” next to each track based only on which options you see."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-2-3","title":"4.2.3 — Duplicate Clip → next slot + seamless switch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.3 — Duplicate Clip → next slot + seamless switch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.2 — Bottom actions fluency</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate the open clip into the next Session slot and keep playback moving on the copy."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> on the Instrument View actions row creates a copy of the currently open clip in the next available clip slot in Session View, and seamlessly switches to playing that clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is the variation factory in one tap: the original loop keeps its take; you land on the copy and can change notes, a pad sample, or an effect without destroying the first idea. You do not drag the clip in Session to get this particular switch — the button both copies and follows."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still need a clip to duplicate. If the track has no clip yet, create one first (<strong>Add Clip</strong> or Capture). Scene order is vertical slots on the same track; “next available” is the next empty slot down that track. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not define what happens if the track already holds eight scenes of clips."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Instrument View on a clip that already has notes. If you have none, add a short Fixed Length clip and play a few pads, then <strong>Add</strong>.","Start playback with <strong>Play/Stop</strong> or by launching the clip.","Tap <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong>.","Return to Session and confirm a copy sits in the next slot on the same track.","Confirm playback has moved to the copy (the playing clip is the new one).","Repeat the duplicate once more from memory, then <strong>Undo</strong> if you do not want the extra copies."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where does the duplicate land? <strong>Answer:</strong> The next available clip slot in Session View (same track).","What happens to playback? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note seamlessly switches to playing the new clip.","Is this the same gesture as long-press → Duplicate on a Session clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Session long-press can Duplicate, but the Instrument View button also switches playback to the copy."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (<strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> → next slot + seamless switch)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (Session long-press Duplicate is a separate clip command)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Duplicate a drum clip once. On the copy only, swap one Drum Rack pad sample from the browser. Play original then copy."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-2-4","title":"4.2.4 — Note Quantize button","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.4 — Note Quantize button","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.2 — Bottom actions fluency</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap the Note Quantize button to snap every note in the open clip to the current quantization settings."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Note Quantize</strong> button aligns notes with a musical grid. Use it when a take was not captured perfectly on beat. It lives on the Instrument View bottom row, not in Set Settings."},{"type":"prose","html":"A single tap quantizes <strong>all</strong> notes in the clip to the current quantization settings. That tap also selects all notes in the clip. A long-press on the same icon opens the <strong>Quantization</strong> tab so you can change the meter value (and <strong>Triplets</strong>) before or instead of relying on the last setting. You can also quantize a smaller selection from the MIDI Editor’s Quantization tab — that path is Level 6."},{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize is a timing edit, not Capture. It does not record new notes. If the result is worse than the take, <strong>Undo</strong>. The next lesson covers how far notes move (the amount control). This lesson is the button itself."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip that has notes slightly off the grid (play a sloppy bar and <strong>Add</strong> if you need one).","Tap <strong>Clip Preview</strong> so you can see note positions, then return to the compact view or stay in the editor.","Tap <strong>Note Quantize</strong> once. Confirm every note moves to the current grid and that all notes become selected.","Tap <strong>Undo</strong>. Confirm the sloppy timing returns.","Long-press <strong>Note Quantize</strong> so the <strong>Quantization</strong> tab opens. Do not hunt for a second Quantize icon.","Repeat: sloppy take → one tap → listen → <strong>Undo</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does one tap on <strong>Note Quantize</strong> do? <strong>Answer:</strong> Quantizes all notes in the clip to the current settings and selects all notes.","How do you open the Quantization tab from that icon? <strong>Answer:</strong> Long-press it.","Is Quantize the same as Capture? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Capture stores what you played. Quantize moves already stored notes to a grid."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (Note Quantize aligns notes to a musical grid)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.3 (tap = all notes at current settings; long-press opens the tab; selects all notes)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture one sloppy 1-bar drum loop. Tap <strong>Note Quantize</strong> and listen. Undo and leave the sloppy version if you prefer the feel."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-2-5","title":"4.2.5 — Quantize amount options","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.5 — Quantize amount options","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.2 — Bottom actions fluency</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set both the grid value and the amount so quantization can be partial instead of a hard snap."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Quantization</strong> tab has two jobs: pick the beat value notes snap toward, and specify <strong>by how much</strong> they snap to that value. Tap a meter button to choose the grid. Tap <strong>OFF</strong> to return the selection to its original position. <strong>Triplets</strong> uses the nearest triplet of the selected meter value."},{"type":"prose","html":"The compiled Note overview describes an <strong>Amount</strong> slider from subtle timing correction to fully rigid snapping. Official release notes list <strong>Quantisation amount</strong> under Note <strong>1.0.15</strong>. The course syllabus labels the same family of features <strong>1.0.16+</strong>. Teach both dates; do not invent a third version number."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The local manual text does not print the slider’s numeric scale (percent or otherwise). Use what you see on your install. Long-press <strong>Note Quantize</strong> to open this tab from Instrument View, or select notes in the MIDI Editor and use the Quantization tab there."},{"type":"prose","html":"A low amount keeps more of the original feel. A high amount is the “on the grid” sound. Choose on purpose."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip with human timing (hats or a melody you played, not a fully quantized loop).","Long-press <strong>Note Quantize</strong> to open the <strong>Quantization</strong> tab.","Choose a meter value (try <strong>1/16</strong>).","Set <strong>Amount</strong> lower than maximum if the slider is present on your version. Tap to apply, or use the editor tab on a selection.","Listen. <strong>Undo</strong>. Raise <strong>Amount</strong> and apply again. Listen for the difference.","Set <strong>OFF</strong> on a selection to hear the original positions return."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two things does the Quantization tab set? <strong>Answer:</strong> The beat value to snap toward, and how much the notes move toward that value.","Which release notes line introduces amount? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note <strong>1.0.15</strong> — “Quantisation amount.” The syllabus says <strong>1.0.16+</strong>.","What does <strong>OFF</strong> do on the tab? <strong>Answer:</strong> Returns the selection to its original position on the grid."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.3 (meter value, amount, <strong>OFF</strong>, <strong>Triplets</strong>; long-press icon)","Ableton Note.md §3.3 (Amount slider: subtle to rigid)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Quantisation amount)","Course syllabus version table (labels 1.0.16)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Quantize the same hat clip twice: once at a low amount, once at full. Keep the version you would actually play in a sketch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-2-6","title":"4.2.6 — Play/Stop global transport","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.6 — Play/Stop global transport","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.2 — Bottom actions fluency</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Start and stop the Set from Instrument View and treat that button as the same transport as Session."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Play/Stop</strong> button on the Instrument View actions row starts or stops <strong>global</strong> transport. It is not a per-track audition key. Session View has its own global <strong>Play/Stop</strong> as well. Either one runs or halts the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"That matters for everything that requires the clock: clip looping, metronome (the click starts when the metronome switch is on <strong>and</strong> you press Play or launch a clip), overdub with <strong>Add</strong>, and automation capture (transport <strong>must</strong> be running). Launching a clip also starts playback on the nearest downbeat; <strong>Stop Clip</strong> on a playing clip stops that clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you are inside Instrument View and the Set feels “stuck,” look at this button before you assume Capture is broken. Stopped transport and running transport are different Capture modes (Level 5)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Enable the metronome in <strong>Set Settings</strong> if you want an audible clock, then return to Instrument View.","Tap <strong>Play/Stop</strong>. Confirm transport runs (click and/or a launched clip).","Tap <strong>Play/Stop</strong> again. Confirm everything stops.","Return to Session. Tap the Session <strong>Play/Stop</strong>. Confirm it is the same clock.","Launch a clip from Session, then enter Instrument View and stop from there.","Start and stop once more from Instrument View without looking at this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Instrument View <strong>Play/Stop</strong> control? <strong>Answer:</strong> Global transport for the Set.","Name two later workflows that require transport to be running. <strong>Answer:</strong> Overdub with <strong>Add</strong> into an existing clip, and capturing automation.","Does launching a clip also start playback? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. A captured clip starts on the nearest downbeat."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (<strong>Play/Stop</strong> = global transport)","Ableton Note Manual §4.5 (Session Play/Stop)","Ableton Note Manual §5.1 (metronome starts with Play or clip launch)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 and §7.5 (overdub and automation need running transport)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With one clip in the Set, start from Session, stop from Instrument View, start from Instrument View, stop from Session. Four taps, one clock."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-2-7","title":"4.2.7 — Duplicate Clip as variation factory","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.2.7 — Duplicate Clip as variation factory","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.2 — Bottom actions fluency</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a second clip that shares the first take, then change only one thing on the copy."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> copies the open clip to the next available Session slot and switches playback to the copy. Treat that as a variation factory: keep the original loop intact, land on the duplicate, change one musical fact."},{"type":"prose","html":"This matches the beatmaking method of not fine-tuning an idea until you decide it is worth continuing. The original clip is the reference. The copy is the experiment — a different hat sample, one extra pad hit after you overdub (Level 5), or a different effect in <strong>Pad FX</strong>. Launching scene rows later lets you A/B those slots as sections."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still cannot invent extra scenes beyond eight, or clips longer than 16 bars. Duplicate does not raise those limits. If you dislike the experiment, the original slot is still there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a clip you would actually keep (a short drum loop is enough).","Tap <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong>. Confirm playback is now on the copy.","Change <strong>one</strong> thing only: swap a single Drum Rack pad sample from the browser, or tweak one effect knob on the copy.","Return to Session. Launch the original clip, then the copy. Listen for the single change.","If the copy is worse, you may <strong>Delete</strong> it from Session (long-press) or leave it muted later. Keep the original.","Duplicate once more from memory and change a different single fact."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why duplicate before you experiment? <strong>Answer:</strong> The button keeps the original clip and switches you onto a copy you can change.","How many things should you change on the first copy in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> One.","What Session limit still applies after many duplicates? <strong>Answer:</strong> Eight scenes / eight clip slots on the grid’s scene axis; clips still max 16 bars."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (<strong>Duplicate Clip</strong> next slot + seamless switch)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (do not over-fine-tune before you decide the idea is worth it; scenes as sections)","Ableton Note Manual §4.3 / FAQ (8 scenes; 16-bar clip max)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make three clips on one drum track: original, copy with a different hat sample, copy with one extra hit (overdub if you already know <strong>Add</strong>; otherwise change only the sample). Launch all three."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-4-3-103","code":"4.3","title":"4.3","blurb":"4.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l4-3-1","title":"4.3.1 — Show Note Names","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.1 — Show Note Names","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.3 — Preferences that change playing</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn on Show Note Names so melodic pads display pitch names while you play."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Show Note Names</strong> lives in app <strong>Preferences</strong>, on the <strong>Pads</strong> page — not in Set Settings and not in Pad Performance Options. Enable the toggle to display the corresponding note names on Note’s pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"This preference is global for the app. It does not change the pitches themselves; <strong>In Key</strong> and the Fourths / Sequential (or 2.x piano) layout still decide which pitches you hit. The labels only make those pitches readable. Note <strong>1.0.12</strong> added the show-name toggles."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use names when you are learning a layout or matching a root from <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>. Turn them off if the extra text gets in the way of the pad surface. <strong>Show Sample Names</strong> is the sister toggle for Drum Rack sample names and is the next lesson."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["From the Sets Library or an open Set, open app <strong>Settings</strong> (three dots) and go to <strong>Preferences</strong>.","Open the <strong>Pads</strong> section. Enable <strong>Show Note Names</strong>.","Open a melodic track in Instrument View. Confirm pitch names appear on the pads.","Play a short phrase while reading the names. Match the Set’s root if <strong>In Key</strong> is on.","Disable the toggle, return to the pads, and confirm the names are gone.","Leave the toggle in the state you want as your default, and say where it lives without looking."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is <strong>Show Note Names</strong>? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Pads</strong>.","Does the toggle change which pitches the pads play? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only displays names.","Which version added the name toggles? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note <strong>1.0.12</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.3 Pads","Note Release Notes 1.0.12 (Show note names and sample names)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With names on, play the root, a fifth, and an octave above the root on a melodic 5×5 (or Keys). Write those three names down."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-3-2","title":"4.3.2 — Show Sample Names","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.2 — Show Sample Names","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.3 — Preferences that change playing</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn on Show Sample Names so Drum Rack pads show which sample each pad triggers."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Show Sample Names</strong> is the second toggle on <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Pads</strong>. Enable it to display the corresponding sample names on the pads. It was added in Note <strong>1.0.12</strong> alongside <strong>Show Note Names</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"On a <strong>Drum Rack</strong> each of the 16 pads holds its own Drum Sampler. Without names you play by position. With names you play by instrument: kick, snare, hat, and whatever the preset actually loaded. The toggle does not load samples or retune them; it only labels what is already there."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can enable both name toggles. Melodic pads care about note names; drum pads care about sample names. If a pad still looks unlabeled, the preset may simply have a short or empty display name — {type:'gap'} the extracts do not describe fallback text when a sample has no name."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Pads</strong>.","Enable <strong>Show Sample Names</strong>.","Open the Drum Rack track in Instrument View.","Read the 4×4. Tap the pad whose name says it is a kick (or the closest labeled low drum), then a hat.","Disable the toggle and play the same two positions by memory of the grid.","Turn names back on if they help you, and point to the preference without this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which pads show sample names? <strong>Answer:</strong> Drum pads, once <strong>Show Sample Names</strong> is on.","Does enabling the toggle change the kit? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It only displays names for the samples already on the pads.","Where is the control? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Pads</strong>, next to <strong>Show Note Names</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.3 Pads","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 / §7.2.2 (Drum Rack = 16 pads, each a Drum Sampler)","Note Release Notes 1.0.12"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With sample names on, write a 4×4 map of the current kit on paper (even if some names are abbreviated). Play a groove using only pads you can name."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-3-3","title":"4.3.3 — Melodic layout: Fourths (perfect fourth vertical)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.3 — Melodic layout: Fourths (perfect fourth vertical)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.3 — Preferences that change playing</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set melodic pads to Fourths and play a vertical perfect fourth on purpose."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong> is a <strong>Preferences</strong> choice, not a per-clip setting. You pick <strong>Fourths</strong> or <strong>Sequential</strong>. In <strong>Fourths</strong>, the pitch of each pad is a perfect fourth (5 semitones) higher than the pad directly below it."},{"type":"prose","html":"That vertical fourth is the reason the layout exists: one finger can walk stacked fourths, and left-to-right movement is a different interval than in Sequential. Combined with <strong>In Key</strong>, the pads still snap to the Set’s scale; the fourths relationship is how the grid is built."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>1.0.15</strong> also lists a Pads layout preference labeled <strong>Note vs Push-style</strong>. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not state that those two labels equal Fourths and Sequential. If your build shows Note / Push-style instead of (or as well as) Fourths / Sequential, use the on-screen names and still test the vertical interval."},{"type":"prose","html":"This preference applies to melodic instruments. Drum Rack 4×4 is a different grid."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong>.","Select <strong>Fourths</strong>.","Open a melodic track. If <strong>Show Note Names</strong> is off, turn it on for this test.","Hold a pad, then play the pad directly above it. Confirm the interval is a perfect fourth (5 semitones), or the in-scale equivalent if <strong>In Key</strong> is on.","Play a short lick that uses at least one vertical pair.","Leave the preference on <strong>Fourths</strong> until the Sequential lesson."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Fourths, what is the interval from a pad to the pad above it? <strong>Answer:</strong> A perfect fourth — 5 semitones.","Where do you choose Fourths? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong>.","Does Fourths apply to Drum Rack pads? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is the melodic-pads layout preference."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.4 Melodic Pads Layout","Ableton Note.md § Preferences (Fourths = perfect fourth vertically)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Pads layout: Note vs Push-style — labels not mapped in extracts)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With Fourths on, play the same three-note shape in two columns. Listen for the parallel fourths the grid creates."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-3-4","title":"4.3.4 — Melodic layout: Sequential","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.4 — Melodic layout: Sequential","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.3 — Preferences that change playing</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Sequential layout and play a scale run that starts at the bottom-left root pad."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sequential</strong> is the other <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong>. Pitches run from left to right, starting from the root-note pad in the <strong>bottom-left</strong> corner, then continue in order onto the rows above."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is a scale-walk grid: adjacent pads are consecutive steps (chromatic if <strong>In Key</strong> is off; scale degrees if <strong>In Key</strong> is on). It is the opposite mental model from Fourths, where the important interval is vertical."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pick one layout as a default so your muscle memory has a home. You can switch later; the preference is app-wide, not per track. {type:'gap'} The extracts do not say Sequential is stored per Set. Treat it as a Preferences setting you chose."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>2.0.1</strong> makes a <strong>piano</strong> layout the default for new melodic tracks. That is a later, version-gated default (next lesson) and does not erase Sequential as a pad-grid option."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong> and select <strong>Sequential</strong>.","Open a melodic track with <strong>Show Note Names</strong> on.","Find the bottom-left pad. If <strong>In Key</strong> is on, that pad is the root.","Walk right along the bottom row, then continue on the row above. Confirm pitches rise in order.","Play the same short lick you used in Fourths, now on Sequential.","Say “bottom-left is the root start” without reading this page."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where does Sequential start? <strong>Answer:</strong> The root-note pad in the bottom-left corner, then left to right, then the rows above.","Is Sequential a per-track Pad Performance Option? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong>.","What does <strong>In Key</strong> change on this grid? <strong>Answer:</strong> Adjacent pads follow the scale instead of the chromatic sequence."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.4 Melodic Pads Layout","Ableton Note.md § Preferences (Sequential: left to right, bottom to top)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (piano default for new melodic tracks — separate fact)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write why you will keep Sequential or switch back to Fourths. One sentence. Leave the preference on your choice."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-3-5","title":"4.3.5 — 2.x Piano layout default","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.5 — 2.x Piano layout default","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.3 — Preferences that change playing</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Recognize that Note 2.0.1 makes piano the default playable layout on new melodic tracks, and know how that differs from the pre-2.x 5×5 default."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The local Note manual (pre-2.0 surface) says melodic instruments use a <strong>5×5</strong> pad grid by default, with <strong>Keys</strong> available as a two-octave keyboard in Pad Performance Options. Note <strong>2.0.1</strong> changes the default: <strong>piano layout is now the default for new melodic tracks</strong>. The same release sets <strong>C Major</strong> as the default key for new Sets and remembers the last used key and tempo."},{"type":"prose","html":"Teach both, dated. If Help reports a version before 2.0.1, expect 5×5 pads until you tap <strong>Keys</strong>. If you are on 2.0.1 or later, a new melodic track may open already on the piano/Keys-style surface. You can still switch <strong>Pads</strong> ↔ <strong>Keys</strong> in <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>In Key</strong> still changes how Keys look: identical rectangles when on, a chromatic piano with sharps and flats when off. The 2.0.1 default is which layout you land on, not a new instrument."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Help</strong> and read the installed Note version.","Create a <strong>+ New</strong> Set (or add a new melodic track).","Open that melodic track. Observe whether you see a 5×5 pad grid or a piano/Keys layout.","Open <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> and switch between <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Keys</strong> once each way.","Check <strong>Set Settings</strong> → <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>. On 2.0.1+, expect <strong>C Major</strong> on a brand-new Set unless a remembered last key applies.","Write one line: “This install is ___. New melodic tracks default to ___.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What did Note 2.0.1 change about melodic defaults? <strong>Answer:</strong> Piano layout is the default for new melodic tracks (and C Major is the default key; last key/tempo are remembered).","What is the pre-2.x manual default? <strong>Answer:</strong> A 5×5 pad grid; Keys is optional.","Can you still switch after 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> still offers <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Keys</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (piano default; C Major; last key/tempo remembered)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (pre-2.x: 5×5 default; Keys = two octaves; In Key vs piano)","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (In Key off by default in the pre-2.x manual)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On this install, start a new Set and screenshot-in-your-head the first melodic surface. Switch it the other way and play the same three notes on both."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-3-6","title":"4.3.6 — More pads on larger screens","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.6 — More pads on larger screens","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.3 — Preferences that change playing</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Expect more visible pads on a larger screen from Note 1.4 on, without inventing a pad count."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>1.4</strong> states: more pads are shown on larger screens. That is the entire official line. An iPad (or a larger iPhone display) can present more of the playable grid at once than a small phone."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not a new instrument and not extra Drum Rack voices beyond the documented 16 pads. It is a display change. The defaults remain <strong>4×4</strong> for Drum Rack and <strong>5×5</strong> for melodic pads in the pre-2.x manual. <strong>Single</strong> still replaces the drum grid with one large velocity surface. <strong>Keys</strong> is still two octaves."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Release notes do not give a number of extra rows, columns, or breakpoints (which logical screen sizes qualify). Do not quote a pad count you were not given. If you own both a phone and an iPad, compare the same Set on both; if you own one device, just notice how many pads are visible on that screen."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm your Note version in <strong>Help</strong> (1.4 or later to see this behavior).","Open a Drum Rack in Instrument View. Count the pads you can see without scrolling, if scrolling exists on your device.","Open a melodic track on <strong>Pads</strong> layout. Count again.","If you have a second device on the same Ableton account, open the same Set there and compare. If not, write “single device — count is what I see.”","Switch the drum track to <strong>Single</strong> and back so you remember that layout still overrides the grid.","State the 1.4 fact from memory: more pads on larger screens — no invented number."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version shows more pads on larger screens? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note <strong>1.4</strong>.","Does that raise the Drum Rack from 16 sounds to something larger? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not in the sources. Drum Rack is still 16 pads.","What number of extra pads do the release notes specify? <strong>Answer:</strong> None. The line is only “more pads.”"],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.4 (more pads on larger screens)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (4×4 and 5×5 defaults; Single; Keys = two octaves)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 (Drum Rack = 16 pads)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the visible pad count on your device for drums and for melodic Pads. Label it “this screen,” not “the spec.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-3-7","title":"4.3.7 — same lick in Fourths vs Sequential","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.3.7 — same lick in Fourths vs Sequential","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.3 — Preferences that change playing</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play one short melody twice — once in Fourths, once in Sequential — and hear that the finger path changes, not the Set’s key."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Fourths</strong> stacks a perfect fourth (5 semitones) above each pad. <strong>Sequential</strong> walks left to right from the bottom-left root, then up through the rows. Same instrument, same <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>, two different maps."},{"type":"prose","html":"The point of this lab is muscle memory, not a better scale. A lick that is three in-scale neighbors on Sequential may be a diagonal or a column on Fourths. If <strong>In Key</strong> is on, both layouts still refuse out-of-scale pitches; you cannot “play a wrong note” in the scale sense, but you can land on a different scale degree than you meant if you keep the old fingering."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>2.0.1</strong> piano default is a third surface. Stay on <strong>Pads</strong> for this lab so the comparison is Fourths versus Sequential only. Pick a permanent preference after you hear both."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Turn <strong>Show Note Names</strong> on. Enable <strong>In Key</strong> in <strong>Set Settings</strong> and choose a root you know (C Major is the 2.0.1 new-Set default).","Set <strong>Preferences</strong> → <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong> to <strong>Fourths</strong>.","Invent a 4–6 note lick. Play it until it is stable. Do not capture yet unless you want a reference clip.","Switch the preference to <strong>Sequential</strong>. Play the <strong>same pitches</strong> (use the names). Notice the new fingering.","Play the old Fourths fingering on Sequential once so you hear it become a different melody.","Choose Fourths or Sequential as your default and leave it there. Write one sentence why."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What stays the same when you switch layouts? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Set’s key/scale and the instrument. Only the pad map changes.","What is the Fourths vertical interval? <strong>Answer:</strong> A perfect fourth (5 semitones).","Where does Sequential begin? <strong>Answer:</strong> Root pad, bottom-left, then left to right and upward."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §2.4.4 (Fourths vs Sequential)","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (In Key snaps melodic pads to the scale)","Ableton Note.md §3.2.2 (In Key Keys: only scale notes)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (C Major default — useful for the lab key)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the preference you chose. Play the lick from memory on that layout only, then stop. Do not flip back and forth after you decide."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-3-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-4-4-104","code":"4.4","title":"4.4","blurb":"4.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l4-4-1","title":"4.4.1 — Default 4×4 Pads grid","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.1 — Default 4×4 Pads grid","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play the Drum Rack on its default 4×4 grid and treat that as the home layout."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drum Racks use a <strong>4×4</strong> grid of playable pads by default — 16 pads, each its own Drum Sampler plus a pad FX send, with one kit-wide effect. That grid is the <strong>Pads</strong> option in <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>. You can leave it and come back; <strong>Single</strong> is the other drum layout, not the default."},{"type":"prose","html":"Sixteen pads is also the instrument limit: there are not 32 drum voices on one Drum Rack. Larger screens (1.4+) may show more pad UI, but the Rack is still 16. <strong>Show Sample Names</strong> turns the anonymous squares into a labeled kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"Play the 4×4 with fingers, not only with Repeat. This layout is how you pick a pad before you open <strong>Single</strong>, and how you build a full beat instead of one surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Drum Rack track in Instrument View.","Open <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> and select <strong>Pads</strong> if it is not already selected.","Count four across and four down. Play each pad once.","With <strong>Show Sample Names</strong> on, find kick, snare, and a hat.","Play an 8- or 16-step groove using at least three pads. No Repeat yet.","Recite: default drum layout is <strong>4×4 Pads</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the default Drum Rack layout? <strong>Answer:</strong> A <strong>4×4</strong> pad grid (<strong>Pads</strong>).","How many pads does a Drum Rack contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> 16.","How do you return to 4×4 after using Single? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> → <strong>Pads</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (4×4 default; switch to Single and back)","Ableton Note Manual §7.1 (Drum Rack = 16 pads)","Note Release Notes 1.4 (more pads on larger screens — display only)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play a kick-snare-hat loop on the 4×4 for one minute. Do not open Single or Repeat."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-2","title":"4.4.2 — Single layout: large velocity-sensitive surface","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.2 — Single layout: large velocity-sensitive surface","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Switch a selected drum pad to Single so you play one sound on a large velocity-sensitive surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Single</strong> appears only on Drum Racks. It replaces the 4×4 with one large, velocity-sensitive performance area. You first select a pad on <strong>Pads</strong>, then switch to <strong>Single</strong>. The 4×4 disappears; that one sound now fills the surface."},{"type":"prose","html":"The compiled overview notes this is especially helpful for nuanced parts (ghost notes on a snare) and for smaller iPhone screens. Velocity still comes from <strong>where</strong> you tap on that surface (upper = louder — next lesson), not from a second pad."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can go back and forth between <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Single</strong> while capturing clips. Repeat still works in Single. Other pads are not gone from the kit; they are just not on this surface until you return to <strong>Pads</strong> and pick another."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the Drum Rack, stay on <strong>Pads</strong> and tap the snare (or whatever pad you want to feature).","Open <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> and tap <strong>Single</strong>.","Confirm the 4×4 is gone and one large surface remains.","Play several hits at different places on that surface. Listen for velocity change.","Switch back to <strong>Pads</strong>, select a hat, then <strong>Single</strong> again.","Return to <strong>Pads</strong> and say the rule: select first, then <strong>Single</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Does Single appear on Drift or Wavetable? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The manual says this option only appears for Drum Racks.","What must you do before Single is useful? <strong>Answer:</strong> Select a pad on the <strong>Pads</strong> layout, then switch to <strong>Single</strong>.","Can you switch Pads ↔ Single while capturing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. The manual says you can go back and forth as needed."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Single; Drum Rack only; select then switch)","Ableton Note.md §3.2.1 (ghost notes; smaller iPhone screens)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Put the snare in Single and play a bar of loud hits mixed with quiet ones. Switch back to Pads and play the rest of the kit."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-3","title":"4.4.3 — Upper half = higher velocity; lower = lower","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.3 — Upper half = higher velocity; lower = lower","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Place taps on the Single surface so the upper half is louder and the lower half is quieter."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On <strong>Single</strong>, taps on the <strong>upper half</strong> of the large area create higher-velocity notes. Taps on the <strong>lower half</strong> create lower-velocity notes. That is the documented velocity map. It is why Single is a performance surface, not just a zoomed pad."},{"type":"prose","html":"Velocity also shows later in the MIDI Editor as color saturation (higher saturation = higher velocity) and as handles on notes. You do not need the editor to play the map — you need your ears and the two halves."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not describe a continuous top-to-bottom curve or extra left/right velocity rules on Single. Teach upper = higher, lower = lower. Do not invent a percentage scale."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this for ghost snares, soft hats, and accents without hunting a second sample."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select a snare (or hat) on <strong>Pads</strong>, then switch to <strong>Single</strong>.","Tap only the upper half several times. Listen.","Tap only the lower half several times. Listen for quieter hits.","Alternate upper and lower in a simple pattern (loud, quiet, loud, quiet).","If you already have a clip, capture or overdub later; for now, just play.","Point to “loud” and “quiet” on the surface from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you tap for higher velocity? <strong>Answer:</strong> The upper half of the Single surface.","Where do you tap for lower velocity? <strong>Answer:</strong> The lower half.","Is this map documented for the 4×4 Pads grid? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The manual states it for <strong>Single</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (upper half = higher velocity; lower half = lower)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.4 (velocity also shown as color saturation in the editor)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play a bar of backbeat snares: lower-half ghosts on the “and”s, upper-half accents on 2 and 4. No editor."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-4","title":"4.4.4 — Selecting a pad then switching to Single","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.4 — Selecting a pad then switching to Single","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Change which sound Single plays by selecting a different pad on the 4×4 first."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Single</strong> does not include a kit picker of its own in the documented controls. The workflow is fixed: select a pad using the <strong>Pads</strong> layout, then switch to <strong>Single</strong>. The large surface plays that pad. To feature a different sound, go back to <strong>Pads</strong>, tap the new pad, then open <strong>Single</strong> again."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is why the 4×4 remains the home layout even when you love Single. Selection happens there. Performance of one sound at many velocities happens on Single."},{"type":"prose","html":"You may switch back and forth while capturing. Per-track Pad Performance state is saved with the Set, so if you leave the track on Single, it can still be Single when you reopen the Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On <strong>Pads</strong>, tap the kick. Switch to <strong>Single</strong>. Play. Confirm you hear the kick.","Switch to <strong>Pads</strong>. Tap a hat. Switch to <strong>Single</strong>. Play. Confirm the surface is now the hat.","Repeat for snare.","Leave the track on <strong>Pads</strong> when you finish unless you have a reason to stay on Single.","Reopen <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> and say the two-step rule aloud."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the documented order? <strong>Answer:</strong> Select a pad on <strong>Pads</strong>, then switch to <strong>Single</strong>.","How do you put a different kit piece on the large surface? <strong>Answer:</strong> Return to <strong>Pads</strong>, select the new pad, then <strong>Single</strong> again.","Can that state persist after you close the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Pad Performance options are per track and saved with the Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (select on Pads, then Single; go back and forth; state saved with the Set)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Feature kick, then snare, then hat on Single, eight hits each. Always select on the 4×4 first."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-5","title":"4.4.5 — Repeat submenu: beat divisions + OFF","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.5 — Repeat submenu: beat divisions + OFF","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Repeat and name every listed rate, including OFF."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Repeat</strong> in <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> opens a submenu of common beat divisions. The Capture knowledge base and the locked course facts list these rates: <strong>1/4</strong>, <strong>1/4T</strong>, <strong>1/8</strong>, <strong>1/8T</strong>, <strong>1/16</strong>, <strong>1/16T</strong>, <strong>1/32</strong>, <strong>1/32T</strong>, or <strong>OFF</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>T</strong> is triplet. <strong>OFF</strong> switches the feature off altogether so a held pad is a single note again. Repeat works in both Fixed Length and Free Length Capture, and on both <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Single</strong>. The same submenu exists on melodic tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not enable Repeat in Preferences. It is a performance option on the track, and its state is saved with the Set. Leaving a track on <strong>1/16</strong> means the next session still rolls until you choose <strong>OFF</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Drum Rack. Tap <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> → <strong>Repeat</strong>.","Read every rate in the submenu. Find <strong>OFF</strong>.","Select <strong>1/16</strong>. Hold a hat pad and listen to the stream.","Select <strong>OFF</strong>. Hold the same pad. Confirm a single note.","Touch each other rate once so you have heard <strong>1/4</strong> through <strong>1/32T</strong>.","Recite the nine choices (eight rates + <strong>OFF</strong>) from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["List the Repeat rates. <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>1/4</strong>, <strong>1/4T</strong>, <strong>1/8</strong>, <strong>1/8T</strong>, <strong>1/16</strong>, <strong>1/16T</strong>, <strong>1/32</strong>, <strong>1/32T</strong>, and <strong>OFF</strong>.","Does Repeat work only in Fixed Length Capture? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It works in Fixed Length and Free Length.","Where does Repeat live? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> → <strong>Repeat</strong> (sidebar / bar by the pads)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Repeat submenu; <strong>OFF</strong>; Pads and Single)","Capture in Note.md (rates 1/4 through 1/32T; works in both Capture modes)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set Repeat to <strong>1/8</strong>, hold a hat for two bars of click, then <strong>OFF</strong>. Do not capture unless you want the roll."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-6","title":"4.4.6 — Repeat while holding pad; switch divisions live","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.6 — Repeat while holding pad; switch divisions live","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hold a pad so it repeats, and change the beat division while your finger is still down."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual’s Repeat workflow is: hold a pad and tap a submenu button to repeat the held notes at that rate, <strong>or switch between different beat divisions</strong> to create rhythmic variations."},{"type":"prose","html":"That second clause is the musical move. A hat that starts at <strong>1/8</strong> and jumps to <strong>1/16</strong> or <strong>1/32</strong> while you hold is a fill, not a new take. You do not need the MIDI Editor to write the roll. Lift the finger and the stream stops. Choose <strong>OFF</strong> when you want ordinary one-shot hits again."},{"type":"prose","html":"Repeat is available while you capture. The notes you hear can be committed with <strong>Add</strong> or <strong>Capture</strong> in Level 5. This lesson is only the live switch."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open Repeat on the Drum Rack. Select <strong>1/8</strong>.","Hold a closed-hat pad. Let a bar of eighths run.","Without lifting, tap <strong>1/16</strong>. Listen to the rate change.","Tap <strong>1/32</strong>, then back to <strong>1/8</strong>, still holding.","Lift. Tap <strong>OFF</strong>.","Repeat the live 1/8 → 1/16 → 1/32 move from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Do you need to lift the pad to change Repeat rate? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Switch submenu buttons while the pad is held.","What is the musical reason to switch live? <strong>Answer:</strong> Rhythmic variation — rolls and fills — without editing notes afterward.","How do you stop Repeat as a feature, not just lift one finger? <strong>Answer:</strong> Select <strong>OFF</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (hold a pad; switch divisions for variations)","Capture in Note.md (Note Repeat while a pad is pressed)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (hats as the place to skip through note divisions)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Over a kick-snare you play with the other hand (or a looping clip if you have one), hold a hat and switch 1/16 → 1/32 → 1/16 for a one-bar fill. No editor."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-7","title":"4.4.7 — Repeat works in Pads and Single","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.7 — Repeat works in Pads and Single","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Prove Repeat is available on both drum layouts so you can roll a hat on the 4×4 or on the large Single surface."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual states Repeat can be used in both the <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Single</strong> layouts. The submenu does not disappear when you zoom to one pad. On <strong>Pads</strong> you can hold any of the 16. On <strong>Single</strong> you hold the one large surface — and that surface still has the upper/lower velocity map, so a roll can be loud or quiet depending on where you press."},{"type":"prose","html":"Melodic Repeat is a later lesson; the same “both layouts” idea appears there as Pads and Keys. Do not assume Repeat is drum-only. Do not assume it is Pads-only."},{"type":"prose","html":"If Repeat seems missing, you are probably not in <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>, or you selected <strong>OFF</strong>."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On <strong>Pads</strong>, set Repeat to <strong>1/16</strong>. Hold a hat. Listen.","Switch to <strong>Single</strong> on that same hat. Hold the large surface. Confirm the roll continues to be available.","On Single, press the upper half, then the lower half, with Repeat still on. Listen for velocity plus rate.","Switch back to <strong>Pads</strong> with Repeat still at <strong>1/16</strong>. Hold a different pad (snare roll).","Set <strong>OFF</strong>.","State the fact: Repeat works in <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Single</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which drum layouts support Repeat? <strong>Answer:</strong> Both <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Single</strong>.","Can Single still change velocity during a roll? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Upper half is higher velocity; lower half is lower. Repeat does not remove that map.","Where do you turn Repeat off? <strong>Answer:</strong> The Repeat submenu → <strong>OFF</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Repeat in Pads and Single; Single velocity map)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Record in your head (or capture if you already can) one bar: 4×4 kick and snare, then a Single hat roll at 1/16. Layout switch is the point."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-8","title":"4.4.8 — 16 Pitches for drum notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.8 — 16 Pitches for drum notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the drum-clip Pitches tools added in Note 1.3 and use them to retune hits after they are captured."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>1.3</strong> lists <strong>16 Pitches for drum notes</strong> among MIDI Editor features (together with tap add/delete, length handles, Triplets, and the rest of that editor pass). The manual’s matching control is the <strong>Pitches</strong> tab, available on <strong>drum clips</strong>. Vertical sliders raise or lower the pitch of individual drum hits. You can swipe to create a smooth pitch transition across a selection, the same gesture style as velocity ramps."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is not a Pad Performance layout. It is an editor tab after notes exist. Transposing from the <strong>Transpose</strong> tab on a Drum Rack does not retune a sample; it moves notes to different pads. <strong>Pitches</strong> is the tab that changes pitch on the hits themselves."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Release notes do not define what the “16” counts (semitones, scale steps, or one slot per pad). Do not invent a range. Use the sliders you see. {type:'gap'} Pitches is not documented as a playable pad-grid mode."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Note <strong>1.3</strong> or later in <strong>Help</strong>.","Capture or <strong>Add</strong> a short drum clip that contains a kick or tom you can hear retune (Level 5 if you still need Capture; you may tap notes into the editor if a clip already exists).","Tap <strong>Clip Preview</strong> to open the MIDI Editor. Select one or more drum notes.","Open the <strong>Pitches</strong> tab. Move a slider. Listen.","<strong>Undo</strong> if you want the original tuning.","Say the version gate and the tab name from memory: 1.3+, <strong>Pitches</strong>, drum clips only."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you retune drum <em>notes</em> after capture? <strong>Answer:</strong> The MIDI Editor <strong>Pitches</strong> tab on a drum clip (1.3+).","What does Transpose do on a Drum Rack instead? <strong>Answer:</strong> It moves notes to different sample pads; octave transpose is disabled.","Is 16 Pitches a Pad Performance Options layout? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not in the sources. It is listed with MIDI Editor features."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.3 (16 Pitches for drum notes — MIDI Editor list)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3 / §8.2.3.5 (Pitches tab on drum clips; sliders; swipe)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.2 (Drum Rack transpose moves pads; octave buttons disabled)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one snare hit, raise pitch, listen, then lower it. Leave a setting you would keep in a sketch. Do not assume a numbered scale."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-9","title":"4.4.9 — Per-track performance state saved with Set","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.9 — Per-track performance state saved with Set","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave a drum track on Single or on a Repeat rate, close the Set, and find those options still there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The state of a track’s <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> is specific to that track and saved with the Set. If one track is on <strong>Keys</strong> and another is on <strong>Pads</strong> with Repeat at <strong>1/8</strong>, those settings remain after you close and reopen the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"For drums that means: <strong>Pads</strong> vs <strong>Single</strong>, and whichever Repeat rate (or <strong>OFF</strong>) you left armed, come back with the Set. You do not reset the sidebar every session unless you choose to. Auto-save writes the Set about every 10–15 seconds, so you do not need a special “save performance state” command."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is per <strong>track</strong>, not a global Preference. Sequential vs Fourths is Preferences. Show Note Names is Preferences. Repeat <strong>1/16</strong> on <em>this</em> Drum Rack is the track."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On the Drum Rack, switch to <strong>Single</strong> on the hat and set Repeat to <strong>1/16</strong>.","Return to Session. Tap the back arrow to the Sets Library (the Set auto-saves).","Reopen the same Set. Open the same Drum Rack.","Confirm <strong>Single</strong> and Repeat <strong>1/16</strong> are still active.","Set Repeat to <strong>OFF</strong> and return to <strong>Pads</strong> so you are not surprised later.","State the rule: performance options are per track, saved with the Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Is Repeat 1/16 a global Preference? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a per-track Pad Performance state stored in the Set.","What happens after you close and reopen the Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> Each track’s Pad Performance options remain as you left them.","Name one setting that is <em>not</em> this mechanism. <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Show Note Names</strong> / <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong> in <strong>Preferences</strong> (app-wide)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (final paragraph: per-track, saved with the Set)","Note FAQ (auto-save every 10–15 seconds)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set two tracks differently (drum Single+1/8, melodic Keys). Close and reopen. Check both before you change anything."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-4-10","title":"4.4.10 — hat rolls with Repeat 1/16 and 1/32","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.4.10 — hat rolls with Repeat 1/16 and 1/32","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.4 — Drum Rack performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Build a hat fill that uses only Repeat at 1/16 and 1/32 — no MIDI Editor drawing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hats are where beatmaking sources tell you to skip through note divisions. <strong>Repeat</strong> is the control that does that in Note: hold the pad, pick <strong>1/16</strong>, then switch to <strong>1/32</strong> while still holding. <strong>OFF</strong> returns you to one-shot taps for the groove."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do this lab on <strong>Pads</strong> or <strong>Single</strong>; both support Repeat. Single adds velocity via upper/lower half if you want the roll to swell. You may capture the result in Level 5; this lesson is successful if you can <em>play</em> the fill in time with a kick-snare (live or from a looping clip)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not open the editor to “draw” 32nd notes. The gate skill is musical Repeat."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Get a kick-snare loop running (play it live, or launch a clip if you already captured one).","Open Repeat. Select <strong>1/16</strong>. Hold the hat for a bar of sixteenths.","On the next bar, switch to <strong>1/32</strong> for half a bar, then back to <strong>1/16</strong>.","Lift, set <strong>OFF</strong>, and play one-shot hats again.","If you use <strong>Single</strong>, place the 1/32 portion on the upper half so the fill is louder.","Repeat the fill from memory without looking at the submenu labels."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which two Repeat rates does this lab use? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>1/16</strong> and <strong>1/32</strong>.","Must you use the MIDI Editor to create the 32nd-note burst? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Switch Repeat while holding the pad.","How do you return to ordinary hat taps? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>OFF</strong>, then tap."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (hold; switch divisions; Pads and Single)","Capture in Note.md (1/16 and 1/32 among the listed rates)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note (hats: skip through note divisions)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play the fill four times in a row over the same kick-snare. Stop when two of the four feel intentional."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-4-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-4-5-105","code":"4.5","title":"4.5","blurb":"4.5","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l4-5-1","title":"4.5.1 — Default 5×5 melodic pads","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.1 — Default 5×5 melodic pads","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play a melodic instrument on the default 5×5 pad grid before you switch to Keys."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Melodic instruments use a <strong>5×5</strong> grid of playable pads by default. That is the <strong>Pads</strong> option in <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>. Drift, Melodic Sampler, and Wavetable all share this pad surface. You can switch to <strong>Keys</strong> and back as needed; 5×5 is the manual’s factory home."},{"type":"prose","html":"Which pitches sit on that grid depends on <strong>Melodic Pads Layout</strong> (Fourths vs Sequential) and on <strong>In Key</strong>. The grid size is 5×5 either way. Note <strong>2.0.1</strong> makes piano the default for <em>new</em> melodic tracks; on that version you may need to tap <strong>Pads</strong> to see 5×5. On larger screens (1.4+) more pads can be visible; do not invent a new default count."},{"type":"prose","html":"Play the 5×5 with fingers first. Repeat and Octave are extras on top of this grid."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic track (bass or lead).","Open <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> and select <strong>Pads</strong> if the track opened on Keys (likely on 2.0.1+).","Count five across and five down.","With <strong>Show Note Names</strong> on, play a short in-scale phrase if <strong>In Key</strong> is enabled.","Switch to <strong>Keys</strong> for one second so you see the other layout, then return to <strong>Pads</strong>.","Recite: default melodic pad grid is <strong>5×5</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the manual’s default melodic layout? <strong>Answer:</strong> A <strong>5×5</strong> pad grid.","What 2.0.1 change might hide that default on a new track? <strong>Answer:</strong> Piano layout is the default for new melodic tracks.","Do Fourths and Sequential change the 5×5 size? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. They change pitch order, not the documented default grid size."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (5×5 default; switch to Keys)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (piano default for new melodic tracks)","Note Release Notes 1.4 (more pads on larger screens)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a 5-note motif on the 5×5 only. Do not open Keys until the next lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-2","title":"4.5.2 — Keys layout: two-octave keyboard","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.2 — Keys layout: two-octave keyboard","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Switch a melodic track to Keys and play it as a two-octave keyboard."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Keys</strong> replaces the Pads layout with a <strong>two-octave keyboard</strong>. That range is the documented span of the Keys surface. Use <strong>Octave</strong> (next lessons) when you need the keyboard higher or lower; Keys itself is two octaves at a time."},{"type":"prose","html":"How the keyboard looks depends on <strong>In Key</strong> in Set Settings. On: a row of identical rectangles (scale notes only). Off: a piano-style chromatic keyboard with sharps and flats. Both are still Keys, still two octaves."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>2.0.1</strong> may open new melodic tracks on piano already. You can still switch back to <strong>Pads</strong>. Keys is a Pad Performance Option, saved per track with the Set — not a Preferences layout like Fourths / Sequential."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic track. Tap <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> → <strong>Keys</strong>.","Play a scale up and down. Notice you have two octaves under your fingers.","Toggle <strong>In Key</strong> in <strong>Set Settings</strong> and return to Keys so you see rectangles versus piano (detail in the next two lessons).","Switch to <strong>Pads</strong> and back to <strong>Keys</strong> once.","Leave the track on <strong>Keys</strong> for the following lessons.","State the span from memory: Keys = two octaves."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Keys replace the 5×5 with? <strong>Answer:</strong> A two-octave keyboard.","Is Keys chosen in Preferences next to Fourths? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> on the track.","How do you reach notes outside those two octaves? <strong>Answer:</strong> The <strong>Octave</strong> submenu (and not by inventing extra octaves on the Keys row itself)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Keys = two-octave keyboard; Pads ↔ Keys)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (piano default on new melodic tracks)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play a two-octave scale on Keys, then the same pitches on Pads. Write which surface felt faster for that scale."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-3","title":"4.5.3 — Keys layout with In Key uses equal rectangles","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.3 — Keys layout with In Key uses equal rectangles","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable In Key and see Keys become a row of identical rectangles that only play the scale."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the <strong>In Key</strong> switch is enabled in <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>, the <strong>Keys</strong> layout appears as a row of <strong>identical rectangles</strong>. There are no black-key gaps. Every rectangle is a scale degree of the root and scale you chose."},{"type":"prose","html":"The compiled overview states the musical consequence: you cannot play a wrong note relative to that scale. Pads also snap to the scale when In Key is on; Keys is just the keyboard-shaped version of the same idea."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>In Key</strong> is off by default in the pre-2.x manual (chromatic). Note <strong>2.0.1</strong> defaults new Sets to <strong>C Major</strong>, so a new 2.x Set may already have a key engaged. If the Keys surface still looks like a piano, In Key is off — that is the next lesson, not a broken layout."},{"type":"prose","html":"If no melodic instruments exist in the Set, In Key options are greyed out."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm the Set has a melodic track. Open <strong>Set Settings</strong> → <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>.","Enable <strong>In Key</strong>. Choose a root and a scale type (C Major is fine).","Open that track’s <strong>Keys</strong> layout.","Confirm you see equal rectangles, not a piano.","Play only those rectangles. Try to find a sharp that is outside the scale — it should not be sitting there as a separate key.","Name the rule: <strong>In Key</strong> on + <strong>Keys</strong> = identical rectangles."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Keys look like with In Key on? <strong>Answer:</strong> A row of identical rectangles (scale notes only).","Where do you enable In Key? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Set Settings</strong> → <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>, not Pad Performance Options.","When are In Key controls greyed out? <strong>Answer:</strong> When the Set has no melodic instruments."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (In Key on → identical rectangles)","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (In Key off by default in the manual; snaps pads; greyed if no melodic instruments)","Ableton Note.md §3.2.2 (impossible to play a wrong note)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (C Major default on new Sets)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With rectangles showing, write a melody that uses only stepwise motion along the row. You cannot play a chromatic wrong note on this surface."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-4","title":"4.5.4 — Keys layout with In Key off uses a piano keyboard","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.4 — Keys layout with In Key off uses a piano keyboard","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Turn In Key off and play Keys as a chromatic piano with sharps and flats."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When <strong>In Key</strong> is off, <strong>Keys</strong> shows a familiar <strong>piano-style chromatic keyboard</strong>, complete with sharp and flat notes. That is the twelve-note Western chromatic set the manual describes for Pad View when the switch is off."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this when the melody needs a note outside the Set’s scale — a passing tone, a borrowed chord tone — without disabling Keys as a layout. You are not changing Fourths vs Sequential here. You are changing whether the keyboard is filtered to the scale."},{"type":"prose","html":"The 2.0.1 piano <em>default</em> is about which layout new tracks open on. This lesson is about the <em>look</em> of Keys when In Key is off: black and white, chromatic. Turn In Key back on when you want the rectangle safety net again."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings</strong> → <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> and turn <strong>In Key</strong> off.","Open the melodic track on <strong>Keys</strong>.","Confirm you see a piano keyboard with black keys (sharps/flats).","Play a chromatic run that would have been impossible on the rectangle row.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> on again and confirm the rectangles return.","Leave In Key in the state your next idea needs, and state both looks from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Keys look like with In Key off? <strong>Answer:</strong> A piano-style chromatic keyboard with sharps and flats.","Does turning In Key off leave the Keys layout? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. You stay on Keys; only the keyboard shape changes.","Why open chromatic Keys on purpose? <strong>Answer:</strong> To play pitches outside the chosen scale."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (In Key off → piano-style chromatic keyboard)","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (In Key off = all twelve chromatic notes)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write two bars that need one out-of-scale passing tone. Play them on chromatic Keys. Then decide whether that tone is worth leaving In Key off."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-5","title":"4.5.5 — Octave submenu","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.5 — Octave submenu","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Use the Octave submenu to shift the Pad View up or down so Keys or Pads reach a new register."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Octave</strong> in <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> opens a submenu of buttons that change the octave of the Pad View. The compiled overview describes it as shifting the pads or keys up or down so you access a wider pitch range. Keys itself is only two octaves at a time; Octave is how you move that window."},{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>1.2</strong> improved default octaves for new bass versus melodic tracks. You may still need Octave when a bass preset opens too high or a lead sits too low. The setting is part of that track’s Pad Performance state and is saved with the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not list how many octave buttons appear or the numbered range (C1–C6, etc.). Use the submenu you see. Do not invent MIDI octave labels."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic track on <strong>Keys</strong> (or <strong>Pads</strong>).","Tap <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> → <strong>Octave</strong>.","Tap a higher octave button. Play the same finger pattern. Confirm the register rose.","Tap a lower octave button. Confirm the register dropped.","Put the track back on a useful default (bass lower, lead higher) using your ear.","Recite: <strong>Octave</strong> moves the Pad View, not the clip’s already captured notes (those use Transpose in the editor)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Octave change? <strong>Answer:</strong> The octave of the Pad View (the playable surface).","Why do you still need it on Keys? <strong>Answer:</strong> Keys is two octaves at a time; Octave slides that window.","Is Octave stored per track? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Pad Performance options are per track and saved with the Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Octave submenu)","Ableton Note.md §3.2.2 (shift pads or keys up or down)","Note Release Notes 1.2 (improved default octaves for new bass vs melodic tracks)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play one motif at two octaves. Leave the track on the octave you would actually use for bass or lead."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-6","title":"4.5.6 — Repeat on melodic Pads and Keys","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.6 — Repeat on melodic Pads and Keys","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hold a melodic pitch so it repeats, and confirm the same rates work on Pads and on Keys."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Melodic <strong>Repeat</strong> works the same way as on Drum Racks. The manual says the feature works with both the <strong>Pad</strong> and <strong>Keys</strong> layouts. Rates are the same list: <strong>1/4</strong>, <strong>1/4T</strong>, <strong>1/8</strong>, <strong>1/8T</strong>, <strong>1/16</strong>, <strong>1/16T</strong>, <strong>1/32</strong>, <strong>1/32T</strong>, or <strong>OFF</strong>. Repeat works in Fixed Length and Free Length Capture."},{"type":"prose","html":"The compiled overview notes a musical use: arpeggiated patterns or rhythmic melodic lines by holding a note. That is Repeat pulsing one pitch (or several held pitches). It is not the 1.2 <strong>Arpeggiator</strong> sidebar — that is the next lesson and is barely specified in the extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"Hold, switch rates live if you want variation, then <strong>OFF</strong> when you need ordinary sustained or one-shot notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On a melodic track, stay on <strong>Pads</strong>. Open <strong>Repeat</strong> and choose <strong>1/8</strong>.","Hold one pad. Listen to the pulsed pitch.","Switch to <strong>Keys</strong> without turning Repeat off. Hold a key. Confirm Repeat still runs.","Switch the rate to <strong>1/16</strong> while holding.","Select <strong>OFF</strong>.","State the fact: melodic Repeat works on <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Keys</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which melodic layouts support Repeat? <strong>Answer:</strong> Both <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Keys</strong>.","Are the rates different from drum Repeat? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Same beat-division list, including <strong>OFF</strong>.","Is Repeat the same control as Arpeggiator? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Arpeggiator is a separate 1.2 sidebar feature."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Repeat on melodic Pads and Keys; same as drums)","Capture in Note.md (rates; both Capture modes)","Ableton Note.md §3.2.2 (arpeggiated / rhythmic lines by holding)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hold a fifth interval (two pads or two keys) at Repeat <strong>1/16</strong> for one bar, then <strong>OFF</strong>. Listen. Do not open Arpeggiator yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-7","title":"4.5.7 — Arpeggiator sidebar","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.7 — Arpeggiator sidebar","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Find the Arpeggiator in the pad sidebar on Note 1.2 or later, and refuse to invent modes the extracts do not list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>1.2</strong> added <strong>Arpeggiator options in the sidebar next to the pads</strong>. That is the full official sentence. It sits with the other Pad Performance controls, not in Set Settings."},{"type":"prose","html":"Repeat can already pulse held notes into rhythmic lines. Arpeggiator is a separate 1.2 feature in the same neighborhood. Do not merge them. Do not import Live’s Arpeggiator device (rates, styles, slots, Hold, etc.) onto Note unless a Note extract names those controls."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Release notes and the local manual extract do not list Note Arpeggiator rates, styles, latch/hold, octave span, or which instruments support it. Open the sidebar on your version, use what you see, and do not document extra parameters in this lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"If Help shows a version before 1.2, this control is not there. Use Repeat instead."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Read the installed version in <strong>Help</strong>. You need <strong>1.2</strong> or later.","Open a melodic track. Open the sidebar next to the pads (<strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>).","Find <strong>Arpeggiator</strong>. Open it.","Write down only the option labels you actually see. Do not copy a Live manual.","Hold a chord or two pads and listen if the feature is clearly on. Set it back to off/disabled if an off state exists on screen.","Recite the sourced fact only: 1.2+, Arpeggiator options, sidebar next to the pads."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When did Arpeggiator appear? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note <strong>1.2</strong>.","Where is it? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the sidebar next to the pads.","Which arp styles do the extracts name? <strong>Answer:</strong> None. That is a gap."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.2 (Arpeggiator options in the sidebar next to the pads)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (sidebar contents in the pre-2.x manual do not list Arp — version gate)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On 1.2+, turn Arpeggiator on for ten seconds over a two-note hold, then off. On older versions, skip and use Repeat <strong>1/16</strong> instead. File any extra labels you saw as personal notes, not course facts."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-8","title":"4.5.8 — Pressure / Y-axis expression on Keys when In Key","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.8 — Pressure / Y-axis expression on Keys when In Key","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Know that from 1.2.1, Keys in In-Key mode can use the Y-axis on supported presets — and stop there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note <strong>1.2.1</strong> added <strong>Y-axis on Keys in In-Key mode for supported presets</strong>. Conditions in that sentence: you are on <strong>Keys</strong>, <strong>In Key</strong> is on (the rectangle row), the preset is one that supports it, and the version is 1.2.1 or later."},{"type":"prose","html":"That is not MPE. Pitch bend is not available. It is also not the Drum Rack Single velocity map (upper/lower half). It is a Keys + In Key expression path."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} The extracts do not name the parameter the Y-axis drives (filter, volume, modulation destination, etc.), whether it is pressure or vertical finger position, or the list of “supported presets.” If you feel a change when you slide up and down a rectangle, that is the feature; do not assign it a destination this course cannot source."},{"type":"prose","html":"Polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI is the sibling 1.2.1 line (next lesson)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Note <strong>1.2.1</strong> or later.","Enable <strong>In Key</strong>. Switch the melodic track to <strong>Keys</strong> so you see rectangles.","Hold a rectangle and move along the Y-axis (up/down the key). Listen for a change.","Try a second preset if the first feels static — the notes say “supported presets.”","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> off so Keys becomes a piano. Do not claim Y-axis still applies; the release note gates it to In-Key mode.","Write the gate in one line: 1.2.1, Keys, In Key, supported presets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What three conditions does RN 1.2.1 name? <strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Keys</strong> layout, <strong>In-Key</strong> mode, and a supported preset (on 1.2.1+).","Is this MPE or pitch bend? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. FAQ: pitch bend is not available. MIDI Input: MPE is not supported.","What destination does Y-axis control? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not stated in the extracts."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (Y-axis on Keys in In-Key mode for supported presets)","Note FAQ (pitch bend not available)","Note MIDI Input.md (MPE not supported)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a supported-feeling preset, hold one rectangle and move Y while a clip or metronome runs. If nothing changes, try one more preset, then stop. Do not invent a mapping."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-9","title":"4.5.9 — Polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.9 — Polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"State what external MIDI can send into Note — including polyphonic aftertouch — and what it cannot."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note records these MIDI input types: <strong>pitch</strong>, <strong>note length</strong>, <strong>velocity</strong>, and <strong>aftertouch, including polyphonic aftertouch</strong>. Note <strong>1.2.1</strong> explicitly calls out <strong>polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"The same knowledge base lists what is <strong>not</strong> supported: <strong>MPE</strong>, <strong>pitch bend</strong>, and <strong>sustain</strong>. Do not tell a student to hook up a sustain pedal or an MPE surface and expect Note to behave like Live."},{"type":"prose","html":"MIDI input arrives from class-compliant USB or Bluetooth controllers, other iOS apps, or a Mac virtual MIDI network (from 1.0.11). Open the track you want; that track is armed automatically. Recording still uses <strong>Capture</strong>, not a separate record-arm button. Mapping (macros, Capture, Start/Stop, Undo) is a later level."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you have no controller, learn the fact and skip the hardware steps."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Read <strong>Help</strong> version (need 1.2.1+ for the poly-AT line; MIDI input itself is 1.0.11+).","If you have a class-compliant keyboard, connect USB or pair Bluetooth under <strong>Settings</strong> → <strong>MIDI Settings</strong> → <strong>Input</strong>.","Enable the device toggle. Open a melodic track.","Play notes. If the controller sends aftertouch or poly AT, hold a chord and press into the keys. Listen. Capture still commits the notes (Level 5).","Do not expect pitch bend, MPE, or sustain to work.","Recite the recorded types and the three unsupported types."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which expression types does Note record from MIDI? <strong>Answer:</strong> Pitch, note length, velocity, and aftertouch including polyphonic aftertouch.","Name three things Note does not take from MIDI. <strong>Answer:</strong> MPE, pitch bend, sustain.","How do you arm a track for the controller? <strong>Answer:</strong> Open that track. It is armed automatically. Capture is still how notes are stored."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note MIDI Input.md (types recorded; MPE / pitch-bend / sustain not supported; arm by opening the track; Capture)","Note Release Notes 1.2.1 (polyphonic aftertouch from external MIDI)","Note FAQ (controllers via USB or Bluetooth; no pitch bend; no MIDI file I/O)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you own a controller, play and Capture one chord with aftertouch if the hardware sends it. If you do not, write the allowed and forbidden MIDI lists from memory."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-10","title":"4.5.10 — Per-track state saved with Set","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.10 — Per-track state saved with Set","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave one melodic track on Keys plus an Octave/Repeat choice, leave another on Pads, and prove both states reload with the Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pad Performance options are <strong>per track</strong> and <strong>saved with the Set</strong>. The manual’s own example: one track on <strong>Keys</strong>, another on <strong>Pads</strong> with Repeat at <strong>1/8</strong>, still that way after you close and reopen."},{"type":"prose","html":"Apply that to this module. Track A can stay on <strong>Keys</strong>, <strong>In Key</strong> rectangles, a chosen <strong>Octave</strong>, Repeat <strong>OFF</strong>. Track B can stay on <strong>5×5 Pads</strong>. You do not get one global “keyboard mode” for the whole Set. Fourths vs Sequential and Show Note Names remain app <strong>Preferences</strong>; they are the exception."},{"type":"prose","html":"Auto-save (~10–15 seconds) writes the Set. You do not export anything to keep layout state."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set the bass (or first melodic) track to <strong>Keys</strong> and shift <strong>Octave</strong> once.","Set the other melodic track to <strong>Pads</strong> with Repeat <strong>1/8</strong>.","Return to the Sets Library. Reopen the Set.","Check both tracks. Confirm each sidebar matches what you left.","Set Repeat to <strong>OFF</strong> on the pulsing track if you do not want surprises.","State the example from the manual in your own words."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["If track 2 is on Keys, does track 3 have to be on Keys? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. State is per track.","Does reopening the Set reset Octave and Repeat? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Those options remain as saved with the Set.","Is Sequential vs Fourths stored this same way? <strong>Answer:</strong> Not in the sources. That pair lives in <strong>Preferences</strong>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (per-track example: Keys vs Pads+1/8 Repeat)","Note FAQ (auto-save ~10–15 seconds)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Label two tracks on paper: “Keys / octave __” and “Pads / Repeat __”. Close, reopen, and tick the labels if they match the app."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-10</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-11","title":"4.5.11 — melody on Pads then same on Keys","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.11 — melody on Pads then same on Keys","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play one melody on the 5×5, then the same pitches on Keys, and choose a surface for Gate D."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pads</strong> (5×5, Fourths or Sequential) and <strong>Keys</strong> (two octaves; rectangles if In Key, piano if not) are two maps of the same instrument. This lab is the melodic twin of the Fourths vs Sequential lick: same notes, new fingering."},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep <strong>In Key</strong> on so both surfaces stay in the scale and you are not debugging chromatic misses. Use <strong>Show Note Names</strong> on Pads and read the rectangle order on Keys. <strong>Octave</strong> the Keys window if the phrase sits outside the current two octaves."},{"type":"prose","html":"You may Capture the better take in Level 5. Success here is being able to perform the phrase on both layouts without looking at this page."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Enable <strong>In Key</strong>. Turn <strong>Show Note Names</strong> on. Stay on a single melodic preset.","On <strong>Pads</strong>, write a 4–8 note melody. Repeat it until it is stable.","Switch to <strong>Keys</strong>. Find the same pitches. Play the melody.","If a note is off the two-octave window, use <strong>Octave</strong>, then play again.","Play Pads → Keys → Pads once more without stopping to think about the map.","Choose the surface you will use for Gate D and leave the track there."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What must stay constant in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> The pitches (and the preset / key). Only the layout changes.","How long is the Keys window? <strong>Answer:</strong> Two octaves; use <strong>Octave</strong> to move it.","Which layout will you take into Gate D? <strong>Answer:</strong> Your choice — but you must have used both."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (5×5 Pads; Keys two octaves; In Key rectangles)","Ableton Note Manual §2.4.3–2.4.4 (names; Fourths / Sequential)","Course syllabus Gate D (Pads vs Keys; Repeat used musically; Instrument View fluid)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Perform the melody twice on your chosen layout as if someone is watching. Then stop. Do not keep flipping."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-11</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l4-5-12","title":"4.5.12 — Gate D assessment","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"4.5.12 — Gate D assessment","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 4 · Module 4.5 — Melodic performance options</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Self-score Gate D on your own Set: Instrument View is fluent, Pads and Keys both got used, and Repeat did something musical."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate D is a checklist, not software. The syllabus requires: <strong>Pads vs Keys</strong>, <strong>Repeat used musically</strong>, and <strong>Instrument View fluid</strong>. Score yourself on this Set, not a Demo."},{"type":"prose","html":"Instrument View fluent means you can enter from <strong>Add Clip</strong>, the <strong>Track</strong> header, or a double-tap; leave with one back arrow to Session; open the browser from a name; and find <strong>Undo</strong>, <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong>, <strong>Duplicate Clip</strong>, <strong>Note Quantize</strong>, and <strong>Play/Stop</strong> without hunting."},{"type":"prose","html":"Pads vs Keys means at least one drum or melodic phrase exists on the default pad grid and the same musical idea (or another) was played on <strong>Keys</strong> or, for drums, <strong>Single</strong> if you are scoring the drum side — the gate text says Pads vs Keys, so a melodic track must have used both <strong>Pads</strong> and <strong>Keys</strong>. Repeat used musically means a heard rate change or a held roll (<strong>1/16</strong> / <strong>1/32</strong> hats, or a pulsed melodic line), not merely opening the submenu."},{"type":"prose","html":"Fail any line, repeat the matching lesson, then rescore. Do not start Level 5 until the three lines pass."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Set you will submit to yourself as the Gate D demo.","From Session, enter Instrument View three ways on three tracks, and return each time with one back-arrow tap.","On a melodic track, play the Gate D melody on <strong>Pads</strong>, then on <strong>Keys</strong>.","On drums or melody, perform a Repeat phrase (hold + rate, or a 1/16–1/32 hat fill).","Duplicate one clip and change one thing on the copy (sample, layout, or a parameter).","Mark pass/fail on paper for the three gate lines. Redo fails now."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the three Gate D lines? <strong>Answer:</strong> Pads vs Keys; Repeat used musically; Instrument View fluid.","May you score a Demo Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Use your own Set.","What do you do with a failed line? <strong>Answer:</strong> Repeat the lesson that teaches it, then rescore before Level 5."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Course syllabus Gate D (Level 4 checkpoint)","Ableton Note Manual §6, §6.1, §8.1.3 (entries, actions, Pads/Keys/Single/Repeat)","Capture in Note.md (Repeat rates)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a three-line pass/fail list with a one-sentence proof under each (what you played, which control). Do not open Level 5 until all three pass."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l4-5-12</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
